  "conclusions": [
    "Lexical module/function/lambda facts are prepared once and reused; scope validation records resolved nonlocal demand.",
    "Annotation flow skips scripts without annotations/regex; when needed, dead/deferred-scope diagnostics require a different flow from ordinary refinement.",
    "Tokenization/parsing, scope diagnostics, abstract interpretation, name binding, lowering and instruction construction have distinct outputs; pass count alone does not prove removable cost.",
    "Entry syntax/lowered wrappers do not survive merely for whole-program fallback; retained lowered expressions serve actual class/annotation/protocol consumers. Imports compile executable code.",
    "Existing named compile-only/fresh observations do not demonstrate a dominant removable frontend stage. Do not launch a compiler/JIT rewrite from this source map.",
    "Core regex contract provides native UTF8 names/subjects, absolute byte captures, independently clamped scalar/byte windows, current tokens and explicit borrowed ownership.",
    "Contract tests exercise cancellation while provider work is blocked and hold funded cleanup before completion, not only entry/exit token checks.",
    "Core contract coverage is distinct from real adapter capabilities, producer safety, backend interruption and backend performance.",
    "Final producer gaps remain separate match/result owners, native UTF8 name enumeration/copy and token-independent cursor construction with cancellable preparation. No core development stop.",
    "Current fnmatch/glob use their own native cancellable wildcard matcher; they are not regex-backed provider clients or a silent fallback for re."
  ],
